For a conical pendulum of string length \(L\) whose string makes a semi-vertical angle \(\theta\) with the vertical, the radius of the horizontal circle is \(r = L\sin\theta\), and

\(T\cos\theta = mg\), \(T\sin\theta = mr\omega^2 = mL\sin\theta\,\omega^2\)

\(\therefore \omega^2 = \frac{g}{L\cos\theta}\) \(\therefore n = \frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{\frac{g}{L\cos\theta}}\)

**The frequency depends on:**

- the **length \(L\)** of the pendulum — \(n \propto \frac{1}{\sqrt{L}}\), so a longer pendulum revolves more slowly;
- the **semi-vertical angle \(\theta\)** through \(\cos\theta\) — as \(\theta\) increases, \(\cos\theta\) decreases and \(n\) increases (equivalently, on the vertical depth \(h = L\cos\theta\) of the bob below the support, since \(n = \frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{\frac{g}{h}}\));
- the **acceleration due to gravity \(g\)**, hence on the location (latitude and altitude).

**It is independent of:**

- the **mass of the bob** — \(m\) cancels between the two force equations;
- the tension in the string, which is itself determined by the above quantities.

Moment of inertia \(I = \sum m_i r_i^2\) depends both on the mass of a body **and** on how that mass is distributed about the axis, so its expression is different for every shape and for every choice of axis. Evaluating it requires knowing the distance of every mass element from the axis.

The **radius of gyration** \(K\), defined by \(I = MK^2\), i.e. \(K = \sqrt{\frac{I}{M}}\), packs all of that distribution information into a **single length** — the distance from the axis at which the entire mass could be imagined concentrated as a point without changing the moment of inertia.

It is useful because:

- it is **independent of the mass**, so it describes purely the geometry of the body relative to the chosen axis, and gives an immediate feel for how far out the mass lies;
- it allows a complicated body to be replaced by an equivalent point mass in rotational problems, exactly as the centre of mass does for translational problems;
- it makes results compact. For example the total kinetic energy of a rolling body is \(E = \frac{1}{2}M\text{v}^2\left(1 + \frac{K^2}{R^2}\right)\), in which the shape enters only through the single pure number \(\frac{K^2}{R^2}\);
- two bodies with the same \(K\) about their axes behave identically in rotation if their masses are equal.
